\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$; the standard number fields have their usual meanings. All additional conventions are stated locally.


\paragraph{Definitions and conventions.} Fix finite numbers of parties, measurement settings, outcomes, and a distribution of joint settings. A correlation is the array $p(\mathbf a\mid\mathbf x)$ of conditional outcome probabilities. The local polytope $P$ is the convex hull of deterministic local response arrays, equivalently the arrays obtained using a shared classical hidden variable. The quantum set $Q$ consists of arrays $\operatorname{Tr}(\rho\bigotimes_i M^{(i)}_{a_i\mid x_i})$ from a shared quantum state and local measurements in the report's framework. The nonsignalling polytope $R$ consists of nonnegative normalized arrays whose marginal at any group of parties is independent of the other parties' settings. A face is the intersection with a supporting hyperplane. The report calls a local face a Bell inequality when its supporting constraint admits a quantum violation.

\paragraph{Source scope and qualifications.} Use the report's meaning of a quantum-violable Bell face; merely calling every supporting local inequality a Bell inequality would make the first question tautological. Minimal local dimensions, pure-state realizations, and rank-one projectors are bundled requirements. The report's historical treatment of Q must not be silently replaced by a modern closure or commuting-operator correlation set.

\paragraph{Catalogue mathematical target.}
\paragraph{Catalogue wording retained for traceability.}
Very little is known about the structure of $P$ and $Q$. Are all faces of $P$ that are not contained in faces of $R$ Bell inequalities? Is the boundary of $Q$ covered by probability distributions arising from projective measurements on pure states in Hilbert spaces of minimal dimension, with all projectors having rank one?

\paragraph{Saved literature context.} The supplied catalogue's assessment dated 2026-08-21 records: The local and nonsignalling sets and many Bell faces are well understood, but the broad structural questions about all relevant faces of the local polytope and boundary representations of the quantum body are not settled in general. The assessment is retained as dated provenance; it is not a new comprehensive literature or open-status certification.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Describe the relevant faces of the local correlation polytope and determine whether quantum boundary correlations have minimal pure-state, rank-one measurement realizations.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath, record 30000178 (OWR-785-004). Catalogue curation under CC BY 4.0; definitions and English exposition added locally.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Entanglement and Decoherence: Mathematics and Physics of Quantum Information and Computation (2005), Oberwolfach Reports, DOI 10.4171/owr/2005/04, pp. 185--255; official report source and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2005/04}.
\item[S3] Official EMS Press PDF of the same Oberwolfach report; PDF pages 27--28, printed pages 211--212.
\url{https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/45978}.
\item[S4] Collins et al., Bell inequalities for arbitrarily high-dimensional systems; reference in the saved dated catalogue literature review.
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0106024}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30000178}

