\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{The smallest nontrivial face test.} Restrict to two settings and two outcomes $\pm1$ per party and write $C=E_{00}+E_{01}+E_{10}-E_{11}$. For deterministic local outputs $a_0,a_1,b_0,b_1\in\{\pm1\}$,
\[
 C=a_0(b_0+b_1)+a_1(b_0-b_1)\in\{-2,2\}.
\]
Convex mixtures thus satisfy $C\leq2$. Nonsignalling arrays can instead have uniform single-party marginals and correlation signs $E_{xy}=(-1)^{xy}$; explicitly put probability $1/2$ on each output pair satisfying $ab=(-1)^{xy}$. This gives $C=4$, so the supporting constraint is not a supporting constraint of the nonsignalling set.

\paragraph{A pure rank-one boundary realization.} On $|\Phi\rangle=(|00\rangle+|11\rangle)/\sqrt2$, take $A_0=Z,A_1=X,B_0=(Z+X)/\sqrt2,B_1=(Z-X)/\sqrt2$. The four expectations give $C=2\sqrt2$. Each measurement has rank-one spectral projections. This is a quantum boundary point: for arbitrary self-adjoint involutions the Bell operator $T=A_0\otimes(B_0+B_1)+A_1\otimes(B_0-B_1)$ satisfies
\[
 T^2=4I-[A_0,A_1]\otimes[B_0,B_1],\qquad \|T\|\leq2\sqrt2,
\]
since each commutator has norm at most two. The same bound for binary contractions follows by adjoining a two-dimensional dilation with involution block matrix $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}A&\sqrt{I-A^2}\\\sqrt{I-A^2}&-A\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. A one-dimensional local Hilbert space cannot violate the local bound, so local dimension two is minimal for this example.

\paragraph{Scope audit.} This reconstructs the elementary CHSH example, not the asserted facial geometry for every finite scenario. In particular an exposed maximizer of one functional does not parametrize the entire quantum boundary; nonexposed points and other outcome cardinalities remain untouched. Neither bundled universal assertion follows.

